.Dd September 3, 2026
.Dt VDOTS-READALONG 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vdots-readalong
.Nd build a scrolling read-along video from a cue list and audio
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl -cues Ar cues.json
.Fl -audio Ar audio.m4a
.Fl -out Ar readalong.mp4
.Op Fl -title Ar T
.Op Fl -chapters Ar chapters.json
.Op Fl -duration Ar SECS
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
renders one frame per cue \(em a scrolling window of the transcript with the
active sentence highlighted, the current section as a header, and a progress
bar \(em then stitches the frames to the narration audio as an MP4.
.Pp
The point is Google Drive: its native video player runs everywhere (web, iOS,
Android), whereas the JavaScript-synced
.Pa article.html
does not run in Drive's HTML preview at all. The read-along is burned into the
frames, so it needs nothing but a video player.
.Pp
.Xr vdots-listen 1 Ic publish
calls this automatically when
.Xr rsvg-convert 1 ,
.Xr ffmpeg 1 ,
and
.Xr python3 1
are all present.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--chapters F"
.It Fl -cues Ar F
Timed cue list as JSON:
.Bq Brq start , stop , text , kind .
Produced by
.Ic vdots-read --cues-json .
.It Fl -audio Ar F
The narration file. Any format
.Xr ffmpeg 1
reads.
.It Fl -out Ar F
Output MP4 path.
.It Fl -title Ar T
Header text shown before the first chapter.
.It Fl -chapters Ar F
Chapter list as JSON
.Bq Brq start , title ;
each heading switches the on-screen section label.
.It Fl -duration Ar SECS
Audio length. Auto-probed with
.Xr ffprobe 1
when omitted.
.El
.Sh OUTPUT
1280x720 H.264 + AAC, faststart, framerate 30. About 1 MB per spoken minute.
.Sh REQUIRES
.Xr rsvg-convert 1
.Pq librsvg ,
.Xr ffmpeg 1 ,
Python standard library only.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ffmpeg 1 ,
.Xr rsvg-convert 1 ,
.Xr vdots 1 ,
.Xr vdots-listen 1 ,
.Xr vdots-publish 1
